= Reader Digest: What This Document Says, in Brief

<ch:reader-digest>

This ~6,200-page document is a comprehensive reference. You do not need to read it in full. This digest gives you the central conclusions, the honest uncertainties, and the one thing worth reading for your situation.

#note-env(title: [The Central Message])[
ME/CFS is a real, biological, multi-system disease, not a psychological condition or deconditioning. Two large genetic studies (DecodeME GWAS, Maccallini 2026 meta-analysis, ~19,000 cases) converge on a single finding: the inherited vulnerability maps to *neuronal circuits*, with no signal in immune cells. The immune, metabolic, endocrine, and gut abnormalities documented throughout are real but appear to be *acquired* (infection-triggered) rather than genetically encoded. This "brain-first" architecture shapes everything: which mechanisms can start the disease, which only amplify it, and where treatment is most likely to help.
]

== What the Causal Hierarchy Concludes

The document's central analytical framework (Chapter @ch:causal-hierarchy) sorts every mechanism into three tiers: *trigger-capable root causes* (can initiate the full syndrome from a healthy baseline), *amplifiers* (worsen and perpetuate but cannot start it), and *downstream consequences* (result from the disease). The practical upshot for treatment:

- *Treating a consequence cannot cure the disease.*
- *Treating an amplifier may reduce severity without enabling recovery.*
- *Only releasing a trigger-capable mechanism — or enough of the locks it establishes — can plausibly restore health.*

Four mechanisms are proposed as trigger-capable root causes: CNS energy crisis, the metabolic safe-mode lock, the GPCR autoantibody cascade, and TRPM3 channelopathy. These are *hypotheses*, tested formally in Chapter @ch:causal-hierarchy-formal.

== Honest Uncertainties

This document is deliberately honest about what is not known:

- The trigger-capable root causes above are **hypotheses**, not established facts. Each is assigned a certainty score.
- Much of the treatment evidence is weak: small studies, open-label designs, or patient-reported surveys.
- The two-hit model (neuronal genetic vulnerability + environmental trigger) is a strong but not proven framework.
- Several claims remain at the level of speculation and are explicitly marked as such.

A cross-document registry (Appendix I and Chapter @ch:proposed-studies) tracks every hypothesis, its certainty, and the study that would confirm or refute it.

== If You Read One Thing

- *Patient or caregiver:* The symptom and management overview in Chapter @ch:core-symptoms, then the treatment chapters in Part III. If you are severe or very severe, start with Chapter @ch:urgent-action-severe.
- *Clinician:* Chapter @ch:diagnostic-criteria (diagnosis) and the mechanism–treatment map (Chapter @ch:mechanism-treatment-map) for a navigational overview of what targets what.
- *Researcher:* The causal hierarchy (Chapter @ch:causal-hierarchy) and its formal test (Chapter @ch:causal-hierarchy-formal), plus the proposed studies (Chapter @ch:proposed-studies).
- *Funder or policymaker:* The research infrastructure and proposed-studies chapters in Part IV.

== How to Use the Rest

The reading guide that follows routes each audience to the parts most relevant to them and includes a symptom-to-chapter quick-reference. Use the table of contents to navigate, and treat Part V (mathematical modeling) as advanced material.

